WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:07.060
JOEL MCKELVEY: Hello,
and welcome to Looker

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.260
and BigQuery--

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.200
Enterprise Digital
Transformation at Sunrun.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.660
I'm Joel Mckelvey with the
Looker team, and joining me

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.282
is Harish Ramachandraiah
from Sunrun.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.490
We're going to be talking
about modernizing your data

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.240
infrastructure and modernizing
your analytics together

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.280
to drive better
business outcomes

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.230
and to accelerate
digital transformation

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.060
in your organization.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.660
Now, this is particularly
important now,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.290
because we know that data-driven
or insight-driven businesses

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.990
are far more likely to succeed
during a time of disruption

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.820
like the one we're seeing now.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.710
They're going to
be more profitable,

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.200
have higher customer
satisfaction and customer

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.940
retention, and
they're going to be

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.970
able to drive better
and more efficient

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:50.360
operations, et cetera.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.800
So it's really key that we
be successful with data.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.140
So then why are more companies
not successful with data?

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.870
And I think it comes down
to-- with all the businesses

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.070
I've spoken with--

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.560
this concern of balancing
the maintenance,

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.870
keeping the lights on,
using the systems that

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:12.940
exist already with the need to
innovate, and update, and build

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.760
new systems or better
systems going forward.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.810
And it comes down to a rapidly
increasing supply of the three

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.080
Vs of volume, variety,
and velocity of data,

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.110
but also, an incredible
demand for data and demand

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.910
for insights in the business.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.550
And keeping up with the
two of those, particularly

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.260
when you're maintaining
legacy systems,

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.910
can be a real challenge.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.040
Good news is that there's hope.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.130
With modern technology, data
lakes and data warehouses

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.650
have effectively
become a single entity.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.005
That is to say, the massively
parallel processing,

00:01:44.005 --> 00:01:46.870
cloud-based database
like BigQuery

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.845
can be both a data lake
and a data warehouse.

00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:55.450
So you no longer need a staging
database, an analytic database,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.860
and maybe a data mart.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.660
All that data can
be consolidated

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.460
in a single location,
which gives you

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.340
a really nice source of
truth for the business,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.940
simplifying data pipelines,
making analytics much more

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.150
real time.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.350
And then when you take a modern
analytics system like Looker

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.380
and you connect it to one of
these modern data warehouses,

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.960
suddenly you've got data
governance at scale.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.820
Data becomes very,
very accessible.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.390
You free up your
data teams to do more

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.520
and to focus more
on that innovation.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.030
And you can operationalize data
in new and very exciting ways

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.440
to drive those
outcomes you need.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.600
What we call these
new ways of using data

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.500
is "data experiences."

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.100
Certainly, all the
organizations I work with

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.700
are looking at modernizing
business intelligence-- getting

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.220
better dashboards and
reporting, making that simpler,

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.700
making that easier.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.520
But there are new
ways to use data

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.340
when you're not spending all of
your time on the infrastructure

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.080
and analytics maintenance.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.110
And these new ways
include putting analytics

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.420
directly in the CRM
or a chat application

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.440
that your teams might be using,
inserting insights directly

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.230
into people's workflows,
right where and when they

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.260
need the most to make
better decisions-- not just

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.450
large-scale decisions
in the organization,

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.030
but even microscale
decisions, say,

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.310
on a factory production line
or something along those lines,

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.550
so making data so
operationalized

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.610
that the people consume it may
not even know that what they're

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.720
seeing is analytics outcome.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.890
And you can even build custom
applications or use data

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.560
in really new, interesting,
and innovative ways

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.770
to monetize data or drive those
outcomes you're looking for.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.640
So one organization that's
been very successful

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.250
driving positive business
outcomes with data

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.820
is Sunrun, led by
Harish Ramachandraiah,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.090
who's with me today.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.240
And he's going to talk a
little bit about his transition

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.760
from an older
infrastructure to where

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.390
he is today, some of the
pitfalls and benefits he's

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.790
seen doing that.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.718
And I think you'll be
really excited to hear

00:03:54.718 --> 00:03:55.510
what he has to say.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.468
So with that, I'm going
to hand over to Harish.

00:03:57.468 --> 00:04:00.085
Harish, take it away.

00:04:00.085 --> 00:04:01.710
HARISH RAMACHANDRAIAH:
Thank you, Joel.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.610
Thanks.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:08.450
So with that said, just a
quick background about Sunrun.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:13.650
Sunrun has been in the
solar industry, number

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.410
one residential solar company.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.540
It's pioneered in the solar
industry for quite some time

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.040
now.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.800
And since then, it has
offered a number of products

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.050
out there, the
latest being battery

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.530
as a service as well
as storage options.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.920
And just to keep it simple,
what we do is essentially

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.110
have the customers
or the consumer

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:41.300
buy electricity at a discounted
price than what the utilities

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.250
would otherwise offer.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.395
But what we offer
is a clean energy,

00:04:45.395 --> 00:04:48.650
environmentally-friendly,
eco-friendly options.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.810
And so that's our mission.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.370
And if you look
at our mission is

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.480
to ensure that we run
the planet by the sun.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.080
So with that said,
you could envision

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.020
any of your company
in similar profile

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.430
to the company where
you've been associated.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:10.850
You may be in the crossroads
of a BI transformation,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:15.420
and Sunrun was no different
a couple of years back.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.830
So you could imagine, most
of the companies out there

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.620
either have a monolithic
transactional system, either

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.000
an SAP or an Oracle
environment, and then

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.450
a number of other platforms
in terms of marketing or sales

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.470
platforms.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:34.700
And counter to that, you
have BI applications,

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.620
where you essentially
have locked

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.770
into a license, or an
infrastructure, or a support

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:43.290
cost, and skill sets,
and so on and so forth.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.767
And we were no
different than that.

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:48.350
Along with that, I think some
of the business questions-- what

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.683
we were trying to address in
terms of the transformation

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:54.230
was, how do I justify the ROI?

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.780
Or how do I do the
transformation yet

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.690
without doing the
disruption in the existing

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:00.920
side of the business?

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.520
And within the
engineering team, we

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.290
were in the dilemma
trying to figure out

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.830
that it isn't as simple as a
lift-and-shift of your data

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.200
sets, all the schemas,
from one environment

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.620
to the other environment,
or importing these pipelines

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.960
from one to the other ecosystem.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.600
And are you really going
to solve the problem?

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.180
And certainly not.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.160
You want to take advantage
of the newer platforms

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.450
with the better architecture,
and so on, so forth.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.250
So how did we solve that?

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:35.490
So we embarked on a journey
two to three years back

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.910
in trying to figure
out, how do we

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:40.640
go about transforming
some of these problems?

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.260
And some of the critical
ones I have noted here,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.160
but I'm sure most of you can
relate to this, as well as it

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.010
resonates among you--

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.180
is time to market.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.790
From a business standpoint,
it was very, very critical

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.300
for us to work in close
proximity with the business

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:05.330
to be able to turn around
some of their analytical needs

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.960
in a very short duration.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.230
And given the situation,
the expectations

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:13.550
was to build, deploy,
and be able to execute

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.010
some of these products
in a very short duration.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.860
And given that we were
operating in a capital-

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:21.770
and labor-intensive market,
it was very critical

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:25.430
for us to be able to turn
around some of these things.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:29.810
Also, the most popular one
about single source of truth--

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.630
given the number of
integration points

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.515
we have, each of
the business units

00:07:35.515 --> 00:07:37.640
would have their own set
of single source of truth,

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.530
or their own versions of it,
and then the timings of it,

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.830
which would never align
and they would never

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:43.740
come to an agreement.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.950
And so we wanted to
take this opportunity

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.770
to ensure that some of
those business rules

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.560
are encapsulated, both at
the physical layer as well

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.790
as the logical layer
for the senior execs

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:59.490
to come into the forum
and be able to align,

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.190
based on the KPI of the
same set of metrics, what

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.500
they would have agreed upon.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.140
And so it was very
prudent for us

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.890
to ensure that that
single source of truth

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:14.840
is in good shape, and further
to that, as the infrastructure.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.210
And for those of us who've
come in from the legacy

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.040
platform or the on-premise side
of it, having a managed service

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:26.390
was the best thing to
have, or-- we love it.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:29.540
Considering that you're
looking at the pipelines,

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:31.790
you're looking at the
infrastructure from a data

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:35.659
warehousing standpoint-- just
data sets at a petabyte scale--

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:37.760
as well as from a
reporting standpoint,

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.059
where you [? only ?]
have certain expectations

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:43.710
to scale up the number of
users, and so on and so forth.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:46.130
And we certainly were--

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.080
we didn't want to be in the
business of capacity planning.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.810
And so that was very
important for us

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:55.710
to be able to autoscale
some of these expectations.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:59.520
The other thing was in terms
of the cost aspects of it.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:01.430
And for us, it
certainly mattered

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.030
to figure out, what are
the kind of transaction

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:06.840
some of these projects
are undertaking?

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.320
And what are the
costs involved in it?

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.400
Are we locked in?

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.990
Is it flexible enough?

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.030
Can we predict some of
these cost aspects of it?

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:16.280
So that tomorrow,
if the business

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.780
needs to do
additional investment,

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:23.810
they have full transparency
about what it is costing us.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:29.120
And that level of visibility,
certainly, would be helping us.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.920
And finally, the
most important one

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:34.110
is certainly the security and
the compliance side of it,

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.230
which is-- we handle
quite a bit of it.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:39.320
There is still a lot to go
there in terms of the DLP,

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:40.830
in terms of PIA.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:43.130
And in the next
subsequent quarters,

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.540
we certainly plan
to address that.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.860
So with that said,
I would not go

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:49.870
into the legacy
aspects of it, but more

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.030
on the current infrastructure
or the architecture, what

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:55.490
we have in place at Sunrun.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.880
So if you look at this, you're
looking from left to right.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.430
And essentially, you
have a combination

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:06.710
of various platform on cloud,
on-premise, as well as formats

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:08.570
of these data sets
in terms of CSV

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.870
to API to JSON, all
kinds of format.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.510
At the same time, you're
looking at the data

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.800
coming from some of these
financial systems like Oracle.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:23.620
We have two ERP environments,
and Workday production data

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:27.310
sets coming from solar panels,
and so on and so forth.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:29.060
And some of these
data sets certainly

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:33.630
have highly time-sensitive from
a investor relationship or Wall

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:37.850
Street information, as well
as Workday, for example,

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.920
as a number of footprints in
terms of productivity, and as

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:45.780
well as traditional reporting,
and so on and so forth.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.100
So as this data makes its way
into the target environment,

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:55.790
which is the cloud storage where
we retain the raw form of it,

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.850
we use a number of components
or variations of this

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:02.970
in terms of Dataproc,
Dataflow, and then informatics

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.270
are predominantly placed in
terms of doing the pipeline.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:09.620
And so the rough form which
arrives at the cloud storage

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.290
is almost like an
audit trail coming out

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:13.280
of the source system.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.060
You could trace back any of
this transaction information.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.880
But subsequent to that
is where we kind of take

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.000
a two-step approach in
terms of staging the data

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.660
and then subsequently
the live table.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:28.640
Between the two steps,
what we essentially do

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.130
is to ensure that
the change data

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:36.350
capture gets handled in a very
seamless manner in stage one.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:39.470
And then subsequently, if there
are any heavy transformation

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.500
of the business rules,
we incorporate it

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.600
in the subsequent
step, which is probably

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:47.287
close to 20% of those
elements get transformed.

00:11:47.287 --> 00:11:49.370
But by and large, we take
advantage of the staging

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:51.540
itself from a
reporting standpoint.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.270
And so between
the two, we ensure

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:56.460
that we are good to go from
a reporting standpoint,

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.070
from a physical layer, and start
building the logical layer.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.760
And along the way,
we also ensure

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.390
that we make the best practice
from a BigQuery standpoint,

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:09.530
whether it's in terms of
[? updation, ?] making sure

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:13.910
that it is cost effective,
as well as establishing

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:14.810
certain joins.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:17.570
Because just in the nature
of BigQuery, in terms of this

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.610
being a distributed
system, we want

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:23.150
to make sure it makes really
sense where it needs to join.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.300
If not, we try to take
advantage of the platform.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:28.900
And then we have
certain homegrown,

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:32.640
open-source
orchestration engine.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.940
And then we have
scheduler from the Airflow

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.700
and other monitoring
tools in order

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:40.400
to maintain, manage
these pipelines.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.290
And subsequently,
it makes its way

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:43.830
from a consumer
standpoint, where

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:49.400
we have Looker, which is our
prominent reporting tool,

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:53.200
not just from the visualization
and report aspects,

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.500
but also, we take
advantage of it

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:57.350
in more like a
data as a service.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:02.460
And that is something which we
are in full thrust as we speak.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.530
Besides Looker,
where I think close

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.500
to 70-odd percent
of the execs rely

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.030
on some of these
reporting aspects

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:12.280
coming out of the Looker,
there are other consumers.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:16.120
There are some cases where
Google Studio makes use of it

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.840
for quick turnarounds, and
POCs, and so on and so forth.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:25.000
The EAI and ML people, again,
tap into the same data sets.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.550
And for them, it is
a curated data sets.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:28.700
It is transformed.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:32.770
There's nothing much they need
to do versus focus on modeling

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:35.980
and take full advantage of
what BigQuery can provide them

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:38.330
from an EAI and ML standpoint.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:42.240
And then the other
consumers are your--

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.830
and some of the
planning systems,

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:48.470
as well as we also provide
the integration hub.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.160
So that's how the current
structure, but if you really

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.860
look at, there are a number
of pipes or the arrow marks,

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.950
which essentially,
they are equally

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.830
prone for certain failures.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:03.190
And so we ensure that these
are well managed, monitored,

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:04.510
and taken care.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:07.930
And to give you a quick preview
of the pipelines, the data

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:11.440
warehouse, I'll just
quickly go over that.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.010
So as I pointed out in
terms of the double click,

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:18.790
any failure in these pipelines
can cost a wrong decision

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:19.340
downstream.

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:22.090
So we want to make sure
that it is well managed.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.630
And so the footprint is closer
around 700-odd pipelines

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:27.040
as we speak.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.860
And it is growing incrementally
almost every month.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:37.460
The other aspect of this is the
type of transactions we handle.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:39.080
It's not just the
volume side of it.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.470
It's more to do
with the complexity

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:46.060
across the various data
sources as well as correlations

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:47.650
of these data sets.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:50.800
In order to write a good data
story for some of the business

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.990
units, you're talking
of integrations

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:56.425
between financial systems,
Salesforce systems,

00:14:56.425 --> 00:14:58.300
as well as [INAUDIBLE]
and so on and so forth

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:02.590
to do some complex analysis from
a cycle time from conversion

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.110
analysis, as well as
the reporting coming out

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:08.570
of the tax and
financial systems.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:12.420
Besides that, we started
at the cadence of 24 hours

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:14.890
in terms of scheduling
these data sets.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.130
That's got reduced almost
to an hour every day.

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:22.380
And so we run these jobs
at a very high frequency

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.830
on most of the
environments, including ERP.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:34.540
Then just to double click
on the BigQuery again,

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:37.960
this is more to talk about
how we manage these slot

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:40.450
allocations from a
resource standpoint.

00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:43.370
Essentially, we have
three segments here--

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:48.250
the ETL, or in our case,
we manage the ELT process,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:50.030
Looker from a
reporting standpoint,

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:54.010
and then the back-end users
who tap into the BigQuery.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.720
These are the three segments.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.180
But we also have the
ability to prioritize.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:03.850
And, in our case, we have Looker
as the most prominent end-user

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:05.780
community to take
advantage of it.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:09.190
And then the other
slots come into picture.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:11.450
So that's the brief approach.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.930
And so far, we haven't
had any challenges

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.820
in terms of resource allocation.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:19.090
And if you have to scale up,
it's a matter of a few minutes.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:21.730
You can certainly
scale up even further.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:25.600
So that's the aspects from a
data warehousing standpoint,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:30.580
and then, finally,
the reporting.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.020
Looker, again, has
come a long way for us.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:35.230
And so, as I said,
it is not just

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:40.070
reporting in terms of
visualization aspects.

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:44.380
We've taken it in terms of
the hub-and-spoke models

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:47.830
within the Looker community,
where we not only enable

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:51.800
the business users to do
self-service reports, not just

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:53.650
the canned or building
those reports.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:57.220
But also, if you really
look at the power users,

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:00.370
we extend our capacity
of development effort

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:01.810
along with the
business community

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:04.690
in terms of enabling
the data models

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.540
as well as exploring
it and establishing

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:10.388
certain metrics which the
central engineering team may

00:17:10.388 --> 00:17:11.680
or may not necessarily address.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.260
But instead, we
have an opportunity

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:18.589
to extend those spoke models
into the business community.

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:22.640
So overall, that has accelerated
not only the development cycle

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.880
but also to bring in some
of the assembly knowledge

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:27.670
which the business units
can bring in and turn

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:32.320
around certain POCs, integrate
some of their data sets, which

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.960
we probably don't
have as Workday,

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:38.080
and have a wider footprint,
and have a protocol to ensure,

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:41.480
besides some of the automation
in terms of testing these data

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:44.990
sets, [? CSEd ?] processing,
and so on and so forth.

00:17:44.990 --> 00:17:48.490
So that's on the reporting side.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.980
And just to take
it further, I just

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.500
wanted to highlight one
of the case studies.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.270
I know there are a
lot of information

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:58.930
about the social media analytics
in terms of sales conversions,

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:02.080
of marketing channels
analysis out there.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.350
And so we thought of sharing
some of the case studies

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.160
from an Oracle
financial standpoint,

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:13.720
where we were having a pre-built
ETL, or a pipeline, as well

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.930
as physical data models,
[? Starsky ?] models,

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.680
as well as a report
coming out of the box,

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:22.520
and something which we
engineered within the Google

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:25.870
platform to ensure that some
of these data sets-- you know,

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:28.120
there are hundreds of
these pipelines, which

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:29.710
are fairly complex in nature.

00:18:29.710 --> 00:18:31.210
As I said, it's not
just the volume.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:35.060
It is the complexity in
integrating various data sets.

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:38.110
And in this example,
the financials

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:42.940
in terms of general ledger,
accounts receivables, payables,

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.700
we take advantage of it in
order to arrive at the profit

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:48.340
and loss statements.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:53.290
And it has been engineered
from the ground up at Sunrun.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.510
The other aspects which
you can even take it

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.940
further is you could
easily integrate this

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:01.180
